+<section id="result-and-exact-source-boundary" class="level1">
+<h1>1. Result and exact source boundary</h1>
+<p>The formalization session’s endpoint criterion selects an actual member of the canonical arithmetic family. This continuation proves one of its previously unproved analytic inputs. For the <strong>unchanged arithmetic measure</strong>, a fixed finite packet <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, and integers <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\ge k\ge3</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a finite constant <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_h</annotation></semantics></math>, independent of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-1"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>n</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\left(\frac{\omega_{h,k,2n}}{\omega_{h,k,n}}\right)^{1/(2n)}
+       \le C_h n.}                                                    \tag{1}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-1">(1)</a></span></p>
+<p>The norms are the original monic polynomial norms for <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>*</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{h,k}=w_h^{*k}</annotation></semantics></math>; the total mass remains <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_h^k</annotation></semantics></math>. No root-location hypothesis is used in this bound. The proof retains a compact-source mass, a lower convolution constant, and both exponential moments. These constants are defined explicitly below; they have not been numerically enclosed.</p>
+<p>For the packet consisting exactly of a hypothetical nonreal off-line quartet, let</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-2"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">⌊</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">⌋</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ q_k=[1+k(m-1)](k+1)^2,
+ \quad
+ L_{h,k}=2\delta[1+k(m-1)](k+1)
+                 \left\lfloor\frac{(k+1)^2}{4}\right\rfloor.             \tag{2}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-2">(2)</a></span></p>
+<p>The existing exterior theorem gives <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{h,k}\le\epsilon_{h,k,N}</annotation></semantics></math> at every admitted degree. Combining (1) with the other session’s endpoint theorem gives</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-3"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><munder><mo>min</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>sinh</mo><mspace width="-0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>log</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\min_{q_k\le N&lt;2q_k}\epsilon_{h,k,N}
+ \le C_hq_k\sinh\!\left(\frac{\mathcal B_{h,k}}{2q_k}\right),\qquad
+ \mathcal B_{h,k}=\log\frac{V_{q_k-1}V_{q_k}}
+                              {V_{2q_k-1}V_{2q_k}}.}                    \tag{3}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-3">(3)</a></span></p>
+<p>Consequently such a quartet forces</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-4"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>arsinh</mo><mspace width="-0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><munder><mo>liminf</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>log</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\mathcal B_{h,k}\ge
+ 2q_k\operatorname{arsinh}\!\left(\frac{\delta k}{2C_h}\right),\qquad
+ \liminf_{k\to\infty}\frac{\mathcal B_{h,k}}{q_k\log k}\ge2.}           \tag{4}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-4">(4)</a></span></p>
+<p>The upper bound for this four-volume quantity is not proved here. In particular, (1) does not establish RH. It completes the polynomial-norm input in (3) on a specific admissible window and leaves the source/relation-volume comparison with its exact maps. Section 9 expresses that remaining quantity as two positive block log-determinants of actual new relation layers.</p>
+<p>The finite quotient, canonical source, residue action and rank-two control are inputs from the preceding analytic notes. The new proofs in Sections 3–7 use only their explicitly defined arithmetic measure. The endpoint theorem is the formalizer’s written result, not attributed to this continuation. Its finite telescopes are reported kernel-checked; the analytic estimates below have written proofs, not new Lean certificates.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="the-base-source-and-both-kinds-of-quotient" class="level1">
+<h1>2. The base, source, and both kinds of quotient</h1>
+<p>Retain the original marked coefficient square</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-5"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>↓</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mo>↓</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi>ℤ</mi></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>j</mi></mover></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>.</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\begin{array}{ccc}
+ G(\mathbb Z)&amp;\xrightarrow{G(j)}&amp;G(\mathbb C)\\
+ p_{\mathbb Z}\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow p_{\mathbb C}\\
+ \mathbb Z&amp;\xrightarrow{j}&amp;\mathbb C.
+\end{array}                                                          \tag{5}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-5">(5)</a></span></p>
+<p>Here <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo><mo>⊔</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>•</mo></msup><mo>:</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(R)=\{\tau\}\sqcup\{r^\bullet:r\in R\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mn>0</mn><mi>R</mi><mo>•</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_R=0_R^\bullet</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>0</mn><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_R(\tau)=0_R</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>•</mo></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_R(r^\bullet)=r</annotation></semantics></math>. The target of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathbb Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite. The construction remains over the original pointed base <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔟</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak b_\tau</annotation></semantics></math>; no new scalar zero is adjoined at a tensor degree or at a polynomial relation layer.</p>
+<p>Keep <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>V</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>Θ</mi></mover><mi>ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_+=[V\xrightarrow{\Theta}\mathscr B]</annotation></semantics></math> in degrees <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0,1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℬ</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>Θ</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=\mathscr B/\Theta V</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=-x\partial_x</annotation></semantics></math>. The source spaces and the Fourier transform remain those of the earlier theta construction. In particular,</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-6"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>ℳ</mi><mi>Θ</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ g(s)=2\xi(s)=s(s-1)\pi^{-s/2}\Gamma(s/2)\zeta(s),\quad
+ \mathcal M\Theta\phi_*=g.                                            \tag{6}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-6">(6)</a></span></p>
+<p>Let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed finite product of zeros of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, with the full order of each selected zero retained, and write <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>deg</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\deg h</annotation></semantics></math>. Define</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-7"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>*</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ v_h=g/h,\quad
+ w_h(t)=\frac{|v_h(1/2+it)|^2}{2\pi},\quad
+ \mu_h=\int_{\mathbb R}w_h(t)\,dt,\quad m_{h,k}=w_h^{*k}.               \tag{7}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-7">(7)</a></span></p>
+<p>The quotient <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_h</annotation></semantics></math> is entire, including at the selected centres. It is not the meromorphic expression evaluated before removing its actual removable factors. For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the same analytic definitions are meaningful, but the finite arithmetic packet is zero. Only the nonempty case is used for the finite Gram inverses below.</p>
+<p>The source map is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-8"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mover><mo>⊗</mo><mo accent="true">̂</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>P</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>⊗</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>ℳ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \mathcal V_{h,k}:\mathbb C[S]\longrightarrow\mathscr B^{\widehat\otimes k},
+ \qquad P\longmapsto P(D_1+\cdots+D_k)(F_h^{\otimes k}),\qquad
+ \mathcal MF_h=v_h.                                                    \tag{8}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-8">(8)</a></span></p>
+<p>The original cyclic module is <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{h,k}=\mathbb C[S]/(\chi_{h,k})</annotation></semantics></math>. Its arithmetic inclusion keeps the Taylor unit:</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-9"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>υ</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>⊗</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>υ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \eta_{h,k}[P]=\upsilon_h^{\otimes k}P(A_k)1,\quad
+ \upsilon_h=j_h(g/h),\quad A_k=\sum_iM_{s_i}.                           \tag{9}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-9">(9)</a></span></p>
+<p>The supplied source identities are</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-10"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mi>𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mi>𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>⊗</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ J^{(k)}\mathcal V_{h,k}=\eta_{h,k}\pi_\chi,\qquad
+ q^{(k)}\mathcal V_{h,k}=\sigma_h^{\otimes k}\eta_{h,k}\pi_\chi.          \tag{10}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-10">(10)</a></span></p>
+<p>The actual norm of (8) is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-11"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><msub><mi>𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \|\mathcal V_{h,k}P\|^2
+ =\int_{\mathbb R}|P(k/2+iu)|^2m_{h,k}(u)\,du.                          \tag{11}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-11">(11)</a></span></p>
+<p>For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal P_n=\mathbb C[S]_{\le n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the monic norm is the quotient norm of the leading-coefficient map</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-12"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><msub><mo>lc</mo><mi>n</mi></msub></mover><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>inf</mo><mrow><msub><mo>lc</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><msub><mi>𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ 0\longrightarrow\mathcal P_{n-1}\longrightarrow\mathcal P_n
+ \xrightarrow{\operatorname{lc}_n}\mathbb C\longrightarrow0,
+ \qquad
+ \omega_{h,k,n}=\inf_{\operatorname{lc}_nP=1}\|\mathcal V_{h,k}P\|^2.
+                                                                         \tag{12}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-12">(12)</a></span></p>
+<p>The arithmetic quotient is the other specified map from the same source:</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-13"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>×</mi><mi>χ</mi></mrow></mover><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub></mover><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>deg</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ 0\longrightarrow\mathcal P_{n-q}
+ \xrightarrow{\times\chi}\mathcal P_n
+ \xrightarrow{\pi_\chi}E_{h,k}\longrightarrow0,\qquad q=\deg\chi,
+                                                                         \tag{13}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-13">(13)</a></span></p>
+<p>for <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\ge q-1</annotation></semantics></math>, with the preceding relation space zero at <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=q-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Multiplication by the monic <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> carries <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal P_{n-q}/\mathcal P_{n-q-1}</annotation></semantics></math> isomorphically to <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal P_n/\mathcal P_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with leading coefficient unchanged. The map <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_\chi</annotation></semantics></math> kills that very relation. Thus (12), (13), and multiplication by <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> specify the relation between the degree-line norm and the arithmetic quotient metric. One norm is not substituted for the other.</p>
+<p>At each admitted original support label <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, the lifted quotient is <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mi>χ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,P)\mapsto(\lambda,[P]_\chi)</annotation></semantics></math>. An original relation has image <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,0)</annotation></semantics></math>; external absence has image <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Its norm before this projection remains available through (11).</p>
+</section>
+<section id="a-quantitative-local-arithmetic-mass-lemma" class="level1">
+<h1>3. A quantitative local arithmetic mass lemma</h1>
+<section id="uniform-polynomial-growth-on-a-fixed-strip" class="level2">
+<h2>3.1 Uniform polynomial growth on a fixed strip</h2>
+<p>There is a constant <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_0\ge1</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T|\ge10</annotation></semantics></math>, the function</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-14"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ f_T(z)=\zeta(1/2+iT+iz)                                                \tag{14}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-14">(14)</a></span></p>
+<p>is holomorphic on and inside the ellipse</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-15"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ z=\frac12(w+w^{-1}),\qquad |w|=8,
+                                                                         \tag{15}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-15">(15)</a></span></p>
+<p>and has modulus at most <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_T=C_0(1+|T|)^6</annotation></semantics></math> there.</p>
+<p>The ellipse has real and imaginary semiaxes <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>65</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">65/16</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>63</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">63/16</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence its image in the <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>-plane lies in <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>55</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>16</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>71</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-55/16\le\Re s\le71/16</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>ℑ</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>65</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Im s-T|\le65/16</annotation></semantics></math>. The pole of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> is outside this set when <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T|\ge10</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+<p>For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Re s\ge1/4</annotation></semantics></math>, use the identity</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-16"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>∞</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \zeta(s)=\frac{s}{s-1}-s\int_1^\infty\{x\}x^{-s-1}\,dx.              \tag{16}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-16">(16)</a></span></p>
+<p>It follows by summing the steps of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">⌊</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor x\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> for <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Re s&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and then by analytic continuation for <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Re s&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\ne1</annotation></semantics></math>. Its integral is bounded by <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\Re s</annotation></semantics></math>, giving <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1+|T|)</annotation></semantics></math> on the indicated part of the ellipse. For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Re s\le1/4</annotation></semantics></math>, the functional equation</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-17"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></msup><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>sin</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \zeta(s)=2^s\pi^{s-1}\sin(\pi s/2)\Gamma(1-s)\zeta(1-s)              \tag{17}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-17">(17)</a></span></p>
+<p>and the vertical-strip gamma estimate give <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O((1+|T|)^{3/2-\Re s})</annotation></semantics></math>. On the stated strip this exponent is below <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>. The common power <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> in (14) is therefore a valid upper bound with one fixed constant. The gamma estimate and functional equation are the classical inputs recorded in DLMF §§5.11 and 25.4; none assumes RH.</p>
+<p>Every disc of radius one about a point of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math> lies inside the ellipse. The maximum principle and Cauchy’s derivative formula therefore also give</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-18"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \sup_{x\in[-1,1]}|f_T&#39;(x)|\le M_T.                                    \tag{18}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-18">(18)</a></span></p>
+</section>
+<section id="propagate-a-nonzero-value-to-the-critical-line-interval" class="level2">
+<h2>3.2 Propagate a nonzero value to the critical-line interval</h2>
+<p>Put</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-19"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>z</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac displaystyle="false"><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ζ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ r_*=(3+\sqrt{13})/2&lt;4,\qquad
+ z_*=-3i/2=\tfrac12(-ir_*+(-ir_*)^{-1}),\qquad
+ c_* =\zeta(2)^{-1}.                                                    \tag{19}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-19">(19)</a></span></p>
+<p>The absolutely convergent reciprocal Dirichlet series gives <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|1/\zeta(2+iT)|\le\zeta(2)</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f_T(z_*)|\ge c_*&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_T=\max_{[-1,1]}|f_T|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+<p>Apply the maximum principle on <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\le|w|\le8</annotation></semantics></math> to <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_T((w+w^{-1})/2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently subtract from its logarithmic modulus the harmonic function linear in <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>log</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log|w|</annotation></semantics></math> with boundary values <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>log</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log m_T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>log</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log M_T</annotation></semantics></math>. Zeros are handled by the usual subharmonic maximum principle. At <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>*</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=-ir_*</annotation></semantics></math> this gives</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-20"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>ϑ</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϑ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>log</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>*</mo></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>log</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ c_*\le m_T^{\vartheta}M_T^{1-\vartheta},\qquad
+ \vartheta=1-\frac{\log r_*}{\log8}&gt;\frac13.
+                                                                         \tag{20}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-20">(20)</a></span></p>
+<p>Here <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c_*&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_T\ge1</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-21"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>3</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ m_T\ge c_*^3M_T^{-2}.                                                 \tag{21}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-21">(21)</a></span></p>
+<p>This is the three-circles argument with every coordinate map and radius fixed. No polynomial approximation to <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> or assumption about its zeros is used.</p>
+<p>Choose a point attaining <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_T</annotation></semantics></math>. By (18), on a one-sided subinterval of length <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_T/(2M_T)\le1/2</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, the modulus is at least <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_T/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-22"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>8</mn><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>9</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>8</mn><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>7</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>42</mn></mrow></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \int_{T-1}^{T+1}|\zeta(1/2+it)|^2\,dt
+ \ge \frac{m_T^3}{8M_T}
+ \ge \frac{c_*^9}{8C_0^7}(1+|T|)^{-42}.                                \tag{22}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-22">(22)</a></span></p>
+<p>For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in[-10,10]</annotation></semantics></math>, the integral in (22) is continuous and strictly positive: otherwise <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> would vanish on an interval and hence identically on its holomorphic domain. Its minimum on that compact interval is positive. Combining it with (22) proves the all-real statement</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-23"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mo>∃</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>42</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\exists c_\zeta&gt;0\quad
+ \int_{T-1}^{T+1}|\zeta(1/2+it)|^2\,dt
+ \ge c_\zeta(1+|T|)^{-42}\quad(T\in\mathbb R).}                         \tag{23}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-23">(23)</a></span></p>
+<p>The exponent <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mn>42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42</annotation></semantics></math> is deliberately an explicit sufficient exponent, not an optimal local mean-square estimate. The proof is a classical analytic propagation argument specialized here to the needed arithmetic interval.</p>
+</section>
+</section>
+<section id="a-lower-envelope-for-the-actual-convolution-after-three-factors" class="level1">
+<h1>4. A lower envelope for the actual convolution, after three factors</h1>
+<section id="local-mass-of-the-unchanged-gh" class="level2">
+<h2>4.1 Local mass of the unchanged <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g/h</annotation></semantics></math></h2>
+<p>The gamma estimate, with a positive lower constant valid also on compact sets, implies</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-24"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ |\Gamma(1/4+it/2)|^2
+ \ge c_\Gamma(1+|t|)^{-1/2}e^{-\pi|t|/2}.                              \tag{24}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-24">(24)</a></span></p>
+<p>Let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_h=\prod_{\rho\in Z}(1+|\rho-1/2|)^{m_\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|h(1/2+it)|\le H_h(1+|t|)^d</annotation></semantics></math>. The entire identity <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=hv_h</annotation></semantics></math> makes this bound valid at selected centres as well: <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|g|\le H_h(1+|t|)^d|v_h|</annotation></semantics></math>. The factors in (6), including <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s(s-1)|^2=(t^2+1/4)^2</annotation></semantics></math>, then give a positive constant <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_h^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> with</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-25"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ w_h(t)\ge c_h^{(0)}e^{-\alpha_0|t|}(1+|t|)^{-2d}
+                   |\zeta(1/2+it)|^2,\qquad \alpha_0=\pi/2.            \tag{25}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-25">(25)</a></span></p>
+<p>Equation (25) is a weaker bound than the available factor <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+|t|)^{7/2-2d}</annotation></semantics></math>; it follows by an inequality, not by changing the density. On <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T-1,T+1]</annotation></semantics></math>, its exponential and polynomial weights are bounded below by <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{-\alpha_0}e^{-\alpha_0|T|}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{-2d}(1+|T|)^{-2d}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Hence (23) proves</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-26"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>42</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \int_{T-1}^{T+1}w_h(t)\,dt
+ \ge c_h^{(1)}e^{-\alpha_0|T|}(1+|T|)^{-B_h},\qquad B_h=42+2d.          \tag{26}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-26">(26)</a></span></p>
+</section>
+<section id="the-positive-compact-convolution-factor" class="level2">
+<h2>4.2 The positive compact convolution factor</h2>
+<p>The function <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_h</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous, bounded, integrable, and positive outside a discrete set. Thus <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>*</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{h,2}=w_h*w_h</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous and strictly positive at every real point. Indeed its integrand is positive almost everywhere; continuity follows from translation continuity in <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^1</annotation></semantics></math> and boundedness of the other factor. Define the <strong>actual</strong> compact quantities</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-27"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>min</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>ϑ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ b_h=\min_{|v|\le1}m_{h,2}(v)&gt;0,\qquad
+ \vartheta_h=\int_{-1}^{1}w_h(v)\,dv&gt;0.                                 \tag{27}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-27">(27)</a></span></p>
+<p>Both are determined by the original density; neither is assigned the value one. Restricting a nonnegative convolution integral gives</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-28"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ m_{h,3}(u)\ge
+ b_h\int_{u-1}^{u+1}w_h(t)\,dt
+ \ge c_h e^{-\alpha_0|u|}(1+|u|)^{-B_h},\quad c_h=b_hc_h^{(1)}&gt;0.
+                                                                         \tag{28}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-28">(28)</a></span></p>
+<p>The omitted part is the nonnegative integral over <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|v|&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and remains part of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{h,3}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use a lower bound, not a new compactly supported density.</p>
+<p>For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\ge3</annotation></semantics></math>, restrict the remaining <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-3</annotation></semantics></math> variables to <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and use (28). Their sum has absolute value at most <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-3</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives the central analytic estimate</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-29"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>ϑ</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.222em"></mspace><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{
+ m_{h,k}(u)\ge
+ c_h\vartheta_h^{k-3}
+ e^{-\alpha_0(|u|+k-3)}
+ (1+|u|+k-3)^{-B_h}
+ \quad(k\ge3,\ u\in\mathbb R).}                                       \tag{29}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-29">(29)</a></span></p>
+<p>All mass and tensor factors are explicit. For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> the factor <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϑ</mi><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta_h^0</annotation></semantics></math> is the empty product. No statement for <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,2</annotation></semantics></math> is inferred by using a negative exponent. The amplification below only needs <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\ge3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+</section>
+</section>
+<section id="upper-moments-and-exact-lower-monic-norms" class="level1">
+<h1>5. Upper moments and exact lower monic norms</h1>
+<p>Fix <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;b&lt;\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math> and retain both one-factor Laplace values</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-30"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>±</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>±</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ M_h(\pm b)=\int e^{\pm bt}w_h(t)\,dt&gt;0.                               \tag{30}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-30">(30)</a></span></p>
+<p>They are finite by the original gamma decay. Fubini and positivity give</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-31"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \int e^{b|u|}m_{h,k}(u)\,du
+ \le M_h(b)^k+M_h(-b)^k.                                                \tag{31}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-31">(31)</a></span></p>
+<p>The elementary exponential-series inequality <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>!</mi><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^j\le j!b^{-j}e^{bx}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\ge0</annotation></semantics></math> yields</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-32"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>!</mi><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo minsize="1.2" maxsize="1.2" stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo minsize="1.2" maxsize="1.2" stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \omega_{h,k,j}
+ \le (2j)!b^{-2j}\bigl(M_h(b)^k+M_h(-b)^k\bigr).                       \tag{32}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-32">(32)</a></span></p>
+<p>The trial polynomial is <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S-k/2)^j</annotation></semantics></math>, whose value on <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=k/2+iu</annotation></semantics></math> is <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^ju^j</annotation></semantics></math>. Its phase has modulus one and remains in the source map; the generator <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=M_S</annotation></semantics></math> has not been shifted to define a different spectral problem.</p>
+<p>For every leading coefficient of modulus one, the exact least squared norm of a degree-<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> polynomial on <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-L,L]</annotation></semantics></math> with Lebesgue measure is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-33"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>!</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>!</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \mathfrak l_j(L)=
+ \frac{2L^{2j+1}}{2j+1}
+       \left(\frac{2^j(j!)^2}{(2j)!}\right)^2.                           \tag{33}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-33">(33)</a></span></p>
+<p>To check all constants, Rodrigues’ formula gives the Legendre polynomial <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_j(x)</annotation></semantics></math> with leading coefficient <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>!</mi><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>!</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2j)!/(2^j(j!)^2)</annotation></semantics></math> and squared norm <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2/(2j+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Its scaled monic version on <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-L,L]</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique minimizer: subtract it from any competing monic polynomial, and the difference has lower degree and is orthogonal to it. For the source coordinate <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=k/2+iu</annotation></semantics></math>, multiply this minimizer by the retained leading phase <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^j</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves (33) at the original type. See also DLMF §§18.3 and 18.5.</p>
+<p>Restricting (11) to <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-L,L]</annotation></semantics></math> and using (29) proves</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-34"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>ϑ</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><msub><mi>𝔩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{
+ \omega_{h,k,j}\ge
+ c_h\vartheta_h^{k-3}
+ e^{-\alpha_0(L+k-3)}(1+L+k-3)^{-B_h}\,\mathfrak l_j(L)
+ \quad(k\ge3,L&gt;0).}                                                    \tag{34}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-34">(34)</a></span></p>
+<p>The observation has the explicit isometric splitting into the inside and outside interval components. Addition recovers the full source function, and their squared norms add. In the split lift each zero component is the supported zero at its label. The outside component has not been declared absent.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="the-norm-window-estimate-is-proved" class="level1">
+<h1>6. The norm-window estimate is proved</h1>
+<p>Set <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=n</annotation></semantics></math> in (34), and use <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=2n</annotation></semantics></math> in (32). The exact finite bound is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-35"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>!</mi><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>ϑ</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>𝔩</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{
+ \left(\frac{\omega_{h,k,2n}}{\omega_{h,k,n}}\right)^{1/(2n)}
+ \le\left[
+ \frac{(4n)!b^{-4n}(M_h(b)^k+M_h(-b)^k)
+       e^{\alpha_0(n+k-3)}(1+n+k-3)^{B_h}}
+      {c_h\vartheta_h^{k-3}\mathfrak l_n(n)}
+       \right]^{1/(2n)}.}                                              \tag{35}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-35">(35)</a></span></p>
+<p>For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\ge k\ge3</annotation></semantics></math>, this is at most <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_hn</annotation></semantics></math>. Here is one explicit sufficient constant, with <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_* =\max\{1,M_h(b),M_h(-b)\}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-36"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>exp</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>exp</mo><mspace width="-0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>exp</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>max</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ϑ</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{
+ C_h=64b^{-2}\exp(\alpha_0)\,2^{B_h/2}
+       \exp\!\left(\frac{B_h}{2\exp(1)}\right)
+       \sqrt{M_*\max(1,c_h^{-1})\max(1,\vartheta_h^{-1})}.
+ }                                                                     \tag{36}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-36">(36)</a></span></p>
+<p>Proof of the uniform estimate: <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{2n}{n}\le4^n</annotation></semantics></math> in (33) gives <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔩</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak l_n(n)\ge2n^{2n+1}/((2n+1)4^n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Also <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>!</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4n)!\le(4n)^{4n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mo>*</mo><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_h(b)^k+M_h(-b)^k\le2M_*^k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+k-3\le2n</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+n+k-3\le2n</annotation></semantics></math>. After taking the <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-th root the factorial and monic factors contribute at most <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64n</annotation></semantics></math>. The mass factors contribute at most the square root in (36); the exponential contributes at most <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>exp</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(\alpha_0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mo>exp</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mo>exp</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{1/n}\le\exp(1/\exp(1))</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>exp</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>exp</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2n)^{B_h/(2n)}\le2^{B_h/2}\exp(B_h/(2\exp(1)))</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves (1).</p>
+<p>The constant is intentionally not optimized. Its dependence on the fixed packet is allowed; its independence of the polynomial and tensor degrees is what is needed. Defining it via positive compact minima does not claim to have provided a numerical interval enclosure for those minima.</p>
+<p>For a general source window the same two inequalities give, without asymptotic notation,</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-37"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>!</mi><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>ϑ</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>𝔩</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \left(\frac{\omega_{h,k,n+r}}{\omega_{h,k,n}}\right)^{1/(2r)}
+ \le\left[
+ \frac{(2(n+r))!b^{-2(n+r)}(M_h(b)^k+M_h(-b)^k)
+       e^{\alpha_0(L+k-3)}(1+L+k-3)^{B_h}}
+      {c_h\vartheta_h^{k-3}\mathfrak l_n(L)}
+       \right]^{1/(2r)}.                                               \tag{37}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-37">(37)</a></span></p>
+</section>
+<section id="compose-the-proved-bound-with-the-formalizers-endpoint-theorem" class="level1">
+<h1>7. Compose the proved bound with the formalizer’s endpoint theorem</h1>
+<p>For a nonempty cyclic packet <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{h,k}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_N</annotation></semantics></math> be its original canonical quotient metric, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_N=\det G_N&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{h,k,N}</annotation></semantics></math> the original rank-two control allowance. The new endpoint theorem supplied by PR #23 is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-38"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>min</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>sinh</mo><mspace width="-0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>log</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \min_{0\le j&lt;r}\epsilon_{h,k,n+j}
+ \le
+ \left(\frac{\omega_{h,k,n+r}}{\omega_{h,k,n}}\right)^{1/(2r)}
+ \sinh\!\left(\frac1{2r}
+       \log\frac{V_{n-1}V_n}{V_{n+r-1}V_{n+r}}\right),\qquad n\ge q.
+                                                                         \tag{38}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-38">(38)</a></span></p>
+<p>Its proof multiplies the local Toda bounds, applies concavity of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>log</mo><mo>sinh</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\sinh x</annotation></semantics></math>, and uses the two exact telescopes. The zero-contraction case is handled before taking logarithms. This result is credited to the parallel formalization/research session. The proof and the distinction between checked finite components and written Jensen assembly are retained in its source.</p>
+<p>Choose <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mo>max</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\ge\max(q,k)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=n</annotation></semantics></math>. Equations (1) and (38) yield the actual canonical selection estimate</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-39"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><munder><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>n</mi><mo>sinh</mo><mspace width="-0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>log</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{
+ \min_{n\le N&lt;2n}\epsilon_{h,k,N}
+ \le C_hn\sinh\!\left(\frac1{2n}
+                 \log\frac{V_{n-1}V_n}{V_{2n-1}V_{2n}}\right).
+ }                                                                     \tag{39}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-39">(39)</a></span></p>
+<p>The chosen index is a member of the existing canonical family. The source representative, Taylor unit, kernel, action and trace are not chosen again after selecting this index.</p>
+<p>For the packet consisting exactly of the quartet in (2), <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_k\ge k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{h,k}/q_k\ge\delta k/2</annotation></semantics></math>. The earlier exterior bound holds at every admitted index, including the one selected by (39). Since <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mo>sinh</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sinh</annotation></semantics></math> is increasing on <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, (4) follows. Moreover <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>arsinh</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>log</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{arsinh}(ak)=\log k+O_a(1)</annotation></semantics></math> for every fixed <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, which proves the stated threshold <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+<p>A proved upper bound with <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>limsup</mo><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>log</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\limsup\mathcal B_{h,k}/(q_k\log k)&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math> would contradict (4). Such an upper bound is <strong>not</strong> an output of this note. The previous sufficient target <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>o</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>log</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal B_{h,k}=o(q_k\log k)</annotation></semantics></math> remains sufficient, but (4) specifies the larger quantitative threshold against which a future upper estimate can be compared. No repeated cost-free exterior operation is used: the multiplicity correction in the new formalization is retained.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="the-remaining-four-volumes-retain-their-finite-jet-maps" class="level1">
+<h1>8. The remaining four volumes retain their finite jet maps</h1>
+<p>The previous source/relation determinant identity remains</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-40"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>𝔇</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔅</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ V_N=\frac{\mathfrak D_{N+1}}{\mathfrak B_{N-q+1}}.
+                                                                         \tag{40}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-40">(40)</a></span></p>
+<p>In the parallel full-jet transfer coordinates (PR #24), let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=N-q+1</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖥</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf F_a</annotation></semantics></math> be its <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2q</annotation></semantics></math> by <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2q</annotation></semantics></math> raw confluent-jet matrix, and let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>𝖵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf V</annotation></semantics></math> be its raw-derivative Vandermonde matrix, with every factorial retained. Its established identity is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-41"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>det</mo><mi>𝖵</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>𝖥</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ V_N=\left(\prod_{j=a}^{a+q-1}\omega_{h,k,j}\right)
+                       \frac{\det\mathsf V}{\det\mathsf F_a}.           \tag{41}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-41">(41)</a></span></p>
+<p>For the window <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=r=q</annotation></semantics></math>, the four transfer indices are exactly <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0,1,q,q+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Put <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_j=\prod_{i=j}^{j+q-1}\omega_{h,k,i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-42"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mo>exp</mo><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>𝖥</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>𝖥</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>𝖥</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>𝖥</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{
+ \exp\mathcal B_{h,k}
+ =\frac{\Omega_0\Omega_1}{\Omega_q\Omega_{q+1}}
+   \frac{\det\mathsf F_q\det\mathsf F_{q+1}}
+        {\det\mathsf F_0\det\mathsf F_1}.
+ }                                                                     \tag{42}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-42">(42)</a></span></p>
+<p>The common Vandermonde cancels in this explicit ratio, not inside any underlying map. The transfer matrices and their inverses still need their actual arithmetic control; the fixed width alone gives no such bound.</p>
+<p>The empty packet has <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_N=1</annotation></semantics></math> as an empty determinant, and no positive-rank inverse or quartet estimate. The analytic inequalities (23)–(37) still apply to its theta seed, but are not statements about a nonexistent finite packet.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="the-volume-window-is-also-two-explicit-retained-boundary-costs" class="level1">
+<h1>9. The volume window is also two explicit retained-boundary costs</h1>
+<p>There is a second useful representation of the same remaining quantity, with no reference-density change. Fix <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, both admissible. In the original monic polynomial basis put</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-43"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>diag</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mi>χ</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ F_{i,j}=[b_{i+1},\ldots,b_j],\qquad
+ \Omega_{i,j}=\operatorname{diag}(\omega_{i+1},\ldots,\omega_j),
+ \qquad b_a=[p_a]_\chi.                                                 \tag{43}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-43">(43)</a></span></p>
+<p>The kernel update is the actual sum <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>Ω</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>*</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_j=K_i+F_{i,j}\Omega_{i,j}^{-1}F_{i,j}^*</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i=G_i^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The matrix determinant lemma gives</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-44"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>det</mo><mspace width="-0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>Ω</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>*</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\frac{V_i}{V_j}
+ =\det\!\left(I_{j-i}+\Omega_{i,j}^{-1}F_{i,j}^*G_iF_{i,j}\right).}       \tag{44}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-44">(44)</a></span></p>
+<p>This determinant has positive real value. Its factors are <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+\lambda_a</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_a\ge0</annotation></semantics></math> are the generalized eigenvalues of the Hermitian pair <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>*</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F_{i,j}^*G_iF_{i,j},\Omega_{i,j})</annotation></semantics></math>. No non-Hermitian matrix is asserted positive in an unspecified Euclidean metric.</p>
+<p>Let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> have columns <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal V_{h,k}p_{i+1},\ldots,
+\mathcal V_{h,k}p_j</annotation></semantics></math>. The original boundary-layer isomorphism is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-45"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔟</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mover><mo>→</mo><mo accent="false">∼</mo></mover><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msubsup><mi>𝔟</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>*</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>𝔟</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>*</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \mathfrak b_{i,j}=T_{i,j}-R_iF_{i,j}:
+ \mathbb C^{j-i}\xrightarrow{\sim}
+ \mathcal D_j\cap\mathcal D_i^\perp,
+ \qquad
+ \mathfrak b_{i,j}^*\mathfrak b_{i,j}
+ =\Omega_{i,j}+F_{i,j}^*G_iF_{i,j}.                                     \tag{45}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-45">(45)</a></span></p>
+<p>Its jets vanish; its highest polynomial coefficients give injectivity. Any new boundary can be reduced by these highest coefficients to an old boundary, and orthogonality then gives surjectivity. These statements prove the displayed map with the same original relation spaces, not only a dimension count. The representative update is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-46"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝔟</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>*</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>*</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ R_j=R_i+
+ \mathfrak b_{i,j}
+ (\Omega_{i,j}+F_{i,j}^*G_iF_{i,j})^{-1}F_{i,j}^*G_i.                    \tag{46}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-46">(46)</a></span></p>
+<p>Every correction in (46) is an original theta boundary. Its split quotient image is <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> at the receiving fibre, and the input relation remains available in (45). Its cochain primitives are the same fixed-order divisions and tensor signs as in the previous cyclic source construction.</p>
+<p>Apply (44) to <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)=(q-1,2q-1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,2q)</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-47"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>a</mi></munder><mo>log</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>a</mi></munder><mo>log</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\mathcal B_{h,k}
+ =\sum_a\log(1+\lambda_a^{(0)})
+   +\sum_a\log(1+\lambda_a^{(1)}).}                                    \tag{47}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-47">(47)</a></span></p>
+<p>In particular</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-48"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mo>Tr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Ω</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo>Tr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Ω</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \mathcal B_{h,k}\le
+ \operatorname{Tr}(\Omega_{q-1,2q-1}^{-1}F^*G_{q-1}F)
+ +\operatorname{Tr}(\Omega_{q,2q}^{-1}F^*G_qF),                          \tag{48}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="equation-number" href="#eq-48">(48)</a></span></p>
+<p>where each <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> has its own indicated source and target from (43). When this trace bound is too large, (47) retains the full logarithmic cost rather than replacing it by its linear upper bound. The analytic norm estimate in (1) does not bound (47); it supplies the other factor in (39).</p>
+</section>
+<section id="deligne-reading-and-what-has-actually-been-transferred" class="level1">
+<h1>10. Deligne reading and what has actually been transferred</h1>
+<p>The six supplied S20 parts were reassembled in this turn. Their individual chunk hashes matched, and the assembled archive has 216,580,466 bytes and SHA-256 <code>e2005bf31e1fcf362765f73c315c855e0f504f24f34d3a0ab188049da522b7c8</code>. This verifies the supplied bytes, not every transcription claim in them.</p>
+<p>I read the TeX for Definition 1.3.5, Lemmas 3.2.10–3.2.12, the tensor argument 3.2.13, and Corollary 3.3.6 in the nested source files. I compared those exact passages with the supplied top-level French/English frozen page records at printed pages 158, 203, and 206. No PDF text extraction or OCR was used. The source review records a genuine inherited transcription fault: old <code>fr_seg2.tex</code> writes a weight bound <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\le2</annotation></semantics></math> at 3.2.10, whereas the controlling page record says <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math>; only the latter, with integrality, implies <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\le1</annotation></semantics></math>. The old file also corrupts the explicit squared-eigenvalue step in 3.2.13. Those old files remain unmodified; they are not used as authority for these comparisons. No claim of a full independent audit of Weil II is made.</p>
+<p>Deligne’s determinant/rank operation and the upper/dual-lower architecture remain attached to the programme’s specified exterior and residue maps. Here the additional estimate is a characteristic-zero analytic estimate for the actual source, (1), followed by the endpoint selection (39). This is not a statement that the new base satisfies Deligne’s finite-field hypotheses. The remaining volume estimate has not been inferred from purity, positivity of a Gram, or the mere existence of the support.</p>
+<p>The new result is therefore a completed analytic component of the current route: its two endpoint monic norms have the required <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_h(q_k)</annotation></semantics></math> scale on the actual quartet window. The other session’s four-volume theorem now leaves the explicitly specified relation-volume cost as the remaining quantitative input on that window.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="proof-and-computation-status" class="level1">
+<h1>11. Proof and computation status</h1>
+<p>Sections 3–7 contain the new written analytic argument. Sections 8–9 contain explicit finite comparisons and a handoff for checking them using the existing quotient and matrix library. The standard complex-analysis, gamma and Legendre facts used in the argument are proved to the extent needed or referenced below. No general novelty claim is made for these classical ingredients.</p>
+<p>The accompanying checker tests finite exact identities: coordinate factors, Legendre extremal norms, the local algebra in the interval argument, convolution moments on declared Laplace models, source quotients, block updates, raw confluent-jet endpoint factors, the volume telescope, and retained support cases. These tests do not certify the analytic inequalities by sampling them. There is no new Lean certificate and no interval-certified value of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_h</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+<p>This continuation makes no remote changes. The integration files are add-only, and the parallel formalization branches and the reader’s existing sources are left untouched.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="references-and-pinned-inputs" class="level1">
+<h1>References and pinned inputs</h1>
+<ul>
+<li>Deligne, P. <em>La conjecture de Weil. II</em>. Publications Mathématiques de l’IHÉS <strong>52</strong> (1980), 137–252. Supplied S20 source and current page-local records; the selected reading and discrepancies are itemized in <code>SOURCE_REVIEW.md</code>.</li>
+<li>NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions: §§25.4 (zeta functional equation), 5.11 (gamma estimates), 18.3 and 18.5 (Legendre polynomials). Public source pages consulted: https://dlmf.nist.gov/25.4, https://dlmf.nist.gov/5.11, https://dlmf.nist.gov/18.3, https://dlmf.nist.gov/18.5.</li>
